# Code owners — ailuracollective/standards
#
# Every file here is load-bearing for every repository in the organisation. A
# change to a heading in a pull request template changes what every contributor
# in every repository is required to write; a change to labels.yml changes what
# every repository can label. There is no file in this repository that is safe
# to change casually.
#
# Every rule below resolves to the same single owner today, because
# @SiddharthaGF is currently the only collaborator with write access, so the
# grouping changes no outcome yet. It is written as though a second owner
# already existed: each group is a real rule that overrides the catch-all, and
# it records which files carry which blast radius, so adding an owner later is
# a one-line change per group rather than a redesign.
#
# Syntax follows gitignore rules and the LAST matching pattern wins, so the
# catch-all comes first and the specific rules follow it. Rules do not
# accumulate: naming a second owner on a later pattern REPLACES the owners of
# the patterns before it for that path, it does not add to them. Put both
# accounts on one line, separated by a space, when both should be asked.
#
# `*` is the catch-all GitHub documents, and it matches at every depth: a
# pattern containing no slash applies in subdirectories too, so this line alone
# owns every file in the repository, present and future. It is used rather than
# the gitignore-looking `/**` because `/**` is ambiguous in this grammar, and an
# ambiguous pattern in a governance file is not worth carrying.

*                       @SiddharthaGF

# CODEOWNERS owns itself. Without this rule, a pull request that removes a
# reviewer's own required approval could be merged by that reviewer, because
# the rule that would have demanded their review is the thing being deleted.
.github/CODEOWNERS      @SiddharthaGF

# Pull request templates — the widest blast radius in the repository. The
# body-structure gate in ailuracollective/actions/pull-request@v2 resolves
# these files from the consuming repository and requires every heading declared
# here to appear in a pull request body. Renaming or removing a heading changes
# the contract for every open and future pull request across the organisation.
.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE/*   @SiddharthaGF

# labels.yml — the label set. Mirrored by hand into every consuming repository's
# manifest AND into the type-labels input of its policy.yml. A change here that
# is not mirrored makes a gate unsatisfiable rather than merely strict.
.github/labels.yml     @SiddharthaGF

# Issue templates — the forms in the issue chooser. Renaming a body field id
# breaks bookmarked and prefilled links, and GitHub drops a label named here
# without an error when the repository does not have it, so label changes in
# these files are invisible until someone files a test issue.
.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/*          @SiddharthaGF

# ISSUE_STANDARD.md — prose, not data. It does not parse as YAML by design and
# is not read by any tool, which means nothing validates the type list against
# the templates. Editing it deserves the same care as editing a template.
.github/ISSUE_STANDARD.md         @SiddharthaGF

# The release files — the only artifacts here that a machine rewrites without a pull
# request, and the only workflow here that holds a write grant. `release.yml` decides
# when a tag exists; the manifest is the current version, and `initial-version` in the
# config decides what the FIRST tag claims. Raising that number above 0.1.0 asserts a
# compatibility guarantee that no release has earned, and every repository that copies
# this one inherits the claim.
release-please-config.json          @SiddharthaGF
.release-please-manifest.json       @SiddharthaGF
.github/workflows/release.yml       @SiddharthaGF

# AGENTS.md — the instructions every agent and contributor reads before touching
# this repository. A wrong claim in here propagates into every repository that
# copies these files.
/AGENTS.md               @SiddharthaGF

# standards.local.example.yml — the schema every adopting repository copies when it
# customizes the standard, and an interface another project implements a checker
# against. Two blast radii rather than one: a wrong field name makes every
# customization record unreadable, and a wrong rule here teaches repositories to
# record deviations in a shape nobody can check.
.github/standards.local.example.yml   @SiddharthaGF
# templates/AGENTS.md — the starting point for an adopting repository's agent
# instructions. It ships UNFINISHED by design, so "it still has TODOs" is correct
# and a completion requirement is deliberately unmet. Filling one in, or deleting
# the banner that says it is a template, turns it into this repository's
# instructions and leaves every future adopter copying a file about us.
templates/AGENTS.md    @SiddharthaGF